{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Pushed as Gerrit 2064 (independent change from a second worktree, not stacked). Held-out TDD: 9 tests, implementer saw only 2; RED baseline 7 RED / 1 pinning-green. Gates: lint rc=0, mypy 626 files clean, complexity new=0 increased=0 with THREE entries going stale (simpler), 3101 pinning tests green with zero assertion edits, net -46 tracked src lines.\n\nMUTATION TESTING FOUND A REAL HOLE IN MY OWN ORACLE, worth recording. Mutation 1 dropped 'repo_root=repo_root' from ONE of the ten _lib_ops call sites and SURVIVED: my behavioural test called _lib_ops._shadow(...) directly, so it proved the shared helper CAN forward a repo_root but never that the call sites DO. A consolidation that rewired nine sites correctly and dropped the tenth would have passed, leaving that one operation composing its shadow snapshot against the wrong repository, silently. Added test_every_library_shadow_call_site_forwards_its_repo_root (an AST completeness check - structural by necessity, since the contract is 'EVERY call site forwards it' and no single runtime path crosses all ten). It now kills that mutation. Mutation 2 (re-declaring _shadow locally instead of importing it - the exact failure mode this ticket exists to prevent) is killed by two tests: the definition count and the identity check.\n\nAC self-contradiction found and fixed before implementing: AC1 required exactly one 'def _shadow' while AC3 required _lib_ops._shadow to remain unchanged. Resolved by consolidating all FOUR sites; recorded in an earlier comment.\n\nImplementation note verified by the implementer: src/rebar/_engine has no __init__.py, so request.py is path-loaded and imported flat as rebar_reconciler.*. It confirmed the shared import resolves under that load (_positive_int.__module__ == rebar._cli._parsers._common) and that the real subprocess entrypoint still runs. This is now a hard module-level dependency on rebar from the engine, unlike __main__.py which wraps its rebar import in a try/except compatibility boundary - flagged as a follow-up consideration, not a blocker."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787374504594989001,"uuid":"b8298bf5-7669-4789-868d-f351da419cb2"}